ject  would be extremely difficult if not  impossible for me and my group on the  other hand we still wanted this large  amount to greatly increase the number of  mutations that we could detect from a  single sample and that's why we went  with the master a platform has been an  outstanding decision for our  laboratories so we brought it in and  actually we brought the machine  in early 2014 and one of my concerns  about NGS is I felt like it could take  us easily over a year to validate it  with master a we brought it in and we  validated it in a few months it went up  quickly it performed robustly and we  really were able to get it up and  functioning and unlive clinically and I  thought a very good short amount of time  and when you consider that my  organization during that same time frame  was working to put in a new laboratory  information system I think that the  staff and my lab did an outstanding job  so what else can I say about this we wit  with the Aqua focus panel that covers  this 300 plus common mutations in KS in  raspy RF and EGFR and because we were  also wanting to add more for metastatic  melanoma we also added a kid extension  that covers some about 85% of the common  mutations in kids and the sensitivity of  this panel is as Darrell mentioned five  to ten percent very little fraction  although I will tell you that now having  worked with this for about well three  years now  there will be occasional cases that you  will be able to see and clearly call  below 5% but that's rare that is to me a  downside is when you get down below 5%  there will be cases that you will not be  able to confidently call the mutation  and just on the bottom and/or the bottom  side of that frame is just what the  analyzer looks like so how's this  project fared well it's done I think  very well it's been I think a boon to  patients in our community because we've  been seeing because they're screening  more patients for early lung cancers  we're seeing a lot of biopsies for lung  but also for colorectal adenocarcinoma  and yes for metastatic melanoma about  200 cases a year each of pulmonary non  small cell carcinoma and about 200  of colorectal carcinoma in about 30  cases a year metastatic melanoma we've  been working with the Aqua focus version  2 panel with the kid extension up until  2015 when we added the extended wrasse  and as dr. Erwin mentioned we have an A  MP poster on that data in 2015 showing  the extended raft and how that did pick  up an additional 15% of mutations that  were considered clinically significant  and we had that panel live again very  quickly so in early in my laboratory  Massa rates been a reliable workhorse  rarely down when we when it does go down  we can talk to the folks of the Geno  they can remotely remote into our system  and sometimes fix things but it's been  really a workhorse that is reliable and  in fact we've added more to it now we're  doing cystic fibrosis testing also we  added that to the platform as well and  when I learned last year that Aegina was  developing the iplex  high sensitivity with the one to two  percent allele fraction detection I was  very excited about this and here's why  I don't know how many in our audience  out there anatomic pathologists that I  am and at a time the anatomic  pathologists have found out both  surgical and psychologies as well as  these other things that I do clinical  pathology wise but all of us have seen  cases like this this is a photo  micrograph from a pleural fluid  psychology cell block a big group of  cells in the middle of the field is a  few cohesive adenocarcinoma cells and a  lot of other things it's got lymphocytes  and some needeth ileal cells in the  background but that's not a lot of tumor  and this is exactly the kind of case  where you look at you go wow I like to  see 10 to 20%  tomber visually when I assess cases  before going to the mass array and this  one really didn't have it but then I get  that phone call from the onco